\ ff-test: lines
\ `."`, `abort"` and `.(` only parse their string and leave the run-time
\ work to primitives of their own, and `compile` parses the word it
\ compiles.
\
\ Each used to be one opcode doing both jobs, telling them apart by
\ STATE or by where it ran from: `abort"` in an immediate word compiled
\ itself into the definition being built and then ran its string as
\ code, `.(` in a definition compiled its string's bytes as instructions,
\ and `compile` copied one cell - half of a two-cell call.

: p 32 emit . ;

\ `.(` prints at once, in a definition too, and compiles nothing.
.( "now" cr
: quiet .( "compiling" 7 ;
cr quiet p cr

\ `."` prints when the word runs.
: greet ." "hello" ;
greet cr

\ `abort"` in a definition throws -2 when it runs ...
: chk abort" "failed" ;
' chk catch p cr

\ ... also in an immediate word, which runs while another word compiles.
: stop-here abort" "stop" ; immediate
: user stop-here 1 ;
user
3 p cr

\ At the prompt it throws at once.
abort" "halt"
4 p cr

\ `compile w` in an immediate word compiles a call to w when it runs,
\ whatever w compiles to.
: seven 7 ;
: lay-seven compile seven ; immediate
: fourteen lay-seven lay-seven + ;
fourteen p cr
see fourteen
: lay-dup compile dup ; immediate
: doubled lay-dup + ;
21 doubled p cr
